# Elasticity: How Sensitive Markets Are to Price and Income

> Elasticity puts a number on sensitivity — how much quantity responds
> when price, income, or a related good's price changes.

[definition]
**Price elasticity of demand (PED)** measures the percentage change in
quantity demanded caused by a one percent change in price. Because
price and quantity move oppositely, PED is negative; by convention we
use its absolute value.
[/definition]

## 1. The PED Formula

@math
PED = \frac{\%\ \Delta Q_d}{\%\ \Delta P}
@/math

If price rises 10% and quantity demanded falls 20%, PED = 20/10 = 2 —
demand is elastic. The **midpoint (arc) method** averages the two
endpoints so the answer is the same whichever direction you measure:

@math
PED = \frac{(Q_2 - Q_1)/\frac{1}{2}(Q_1 + Q_2)}{(P_2 - P_1)/\frac{1}{2}(P_1 + P_2)}
@/math

## 2. The Five Degrees of Elasticity

| Value of PED | Name | Demand curve shape | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| PED = 0 | Perfectly inelastic | Vertical line | Life-saving medicine |
| 0 < PED < 1 | Inelastic | Steep | Salt, fuel, rice |
| PED = 1 | Unitary elastic | Rectangular hyperbola | Hypothetical benchmark |
| PED > 1 | Elastic | Flat | Restaurant meals, air travel |
| PED = ∞ | Perfectly elastic | Horizontal line | One farmer's wheat in a market |

## 3. What Makes Demand Elastic?

- **Availability of substitutes** — the single biggest factor; many
  close substitutes mean buyers leave when price rises.
- **Necessity vs luxury** — a heart patient's medicine vs a cruise.
- **Share of the budget** — a rise in matchbox prices is ignored; a
  rise in house prices is not.
- **Time horizon** — demand is more elastic in the long run (habits
  and durable stocks adjust slowly).

[example]
Salt has almost no substitutes, costs a tiny budget share, and is a
necessity — so its PED is close to zero. Air travel is a luxury with
substitutes (trains, video calls) and takes a large budget bite —
highly elastic, and airlines discount fares to fill seats.
[/example]

## 4. Elasticity and Total Revenue

@math
TR = P \times Q
@/math

| Demand | Price rises | Price falls |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Inelastic (PED < 1) | TR rises | TR falls |
| Unitary (PED = 1) | TR unchanged | TR unchanged |
| Elastic (PED > 1) | TR falls | TR rises |

[important title="The pricing rule sellers live by"]
When demand is inelastic, raising price raises revenue — which is why
governments tax fuel, tobacco, and liquor. When demand is elastic, a
price cut pays for itself through extra volume.
[/important]

## 5. Income Elasticity of Demand (YED)

@math
YED = \frac{\%\ \Delta Q_d}{\%\ \Delta Income}
@/math

- **Normal goods** — YED > 0. If YED > 1 they are **luxuries**
  (cars, eating out); 0 < YED < 1 are **necessities** (food grains).
- **Inferior goods** — YED < 0: as income rises, demand falls
  (coarse grains, generic brands, bus travel replacing some journeys).

Engel's observation: the share of income spent on food falls as a
household becomes richer — a mark of necessity behaviour.

## 6. Cross Elasticity of Demand (XED)

@math
XED = \frac{\%\ \Delta Q_d\ of\ X}{\%\ \Delta P\ of\ Y}
@/math

| XED value | Relationship | Example pair |
| --- | --- | --- |
| XED > 0 | Substitutes | Tea and coffee |
| XED < 0 | Complements | Petrol and cars |
| XED = 0 | Unrelated | Chalk and cheese |

A high positive XED is how regulators detect "relevant markets" in
competition cases — products that constrain each other's pricing.

## 7. Elasticity Ladder in Practice

Approximate long-run elasticities illustrate the spread of real-world
sensitivity — necessities cluster near zero, discretionary categories
stretch well above one:

